thor":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:42:27.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:42:27.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2739","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2739"}},{"id":"2740","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3814","description":"Burried in the first drop below Caton Creek.","caption":"Burried!","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:42:27.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:42:27.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2740","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2740"}},{"id":"2737","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3814","description":"The road can be seen just above these boaters.  It is usually closer, but does not intrude.","caption":"EFSF Salmon is Beautiful","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:42:27.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:42:27.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2737","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2737"}},{"id":"893234","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896867","description":"An easy take-out where the Secesh joins the South Fork Salmon. This is also the put-in for the multi-day trip down the South Fork Salmon.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n","caption":"Take-out at Secesh","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2020-06-28T05:43:36.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:43:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893234","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893234"}},{"id":"893232","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896867","description":"\t\t\r\n\r\n\r\n","caption":"Rapids below Deadman","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2020-06-28T05:43:36.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:43:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893232","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893232"}},{"id":"893233","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896867","description":"Driving up the East Fork South Fork and scouting on the way.\r\n\r\n","caption":"Shuttle Road Scout","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Thomas O'Keefe ©","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2020-06-28T05:43:36.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:43:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893233","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893233"}},{"id":"2057","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3763","description":"","caption":"Flight Simulator","poi_name":"Flight Simulator","subject":"Liam? Leading - David Schroeder Following","author":"Clay Slaughter","poi_id":"107571","photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:41:41.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:41:41.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2057","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2057"}},{"id":"2058","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3763","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"Flight Simulator","subject":"David Schroeder","author":"Clay Slaughter","poi_id":"107571","photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:41:41.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:41:41.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2058","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2058"}},{"id":"2738","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3814","description":"This is a good lunch spot.  It has plenty of parking, but skimpy camping.  Sit in the ladies toidy with the door open and look accross the river to see Osprey nesting.","caption":"Beach at Deadman Campground","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:42:27.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:42:27.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2738","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2738"}},{"id":"2540","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3814","description":"EFSF Salmon <a href=\"http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/615\">(Reach 615)</a> . . . Upper EFSF Salomn <a href=\"http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/616\">(Reach 616)</a> . . . Johnson Creek <a href=\"http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/562\">(Reach 562)</a> \r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThere are four ways to get to Yellow Pine (counting the airport), but the easiest is from the town of Cascade on Idaho State 55.  Just north of town turn east toward Warm Lake.  Go about 23 miles and turn left toward Krassel RS.  Go down the South Fork Salmon 30 miles.  When the road turns to gravel turn right and go 14.5 miles up the East Fork of the South Fork to the intersection of Yellow Pine Ave, Stibnite Road and Johnson Creek Road.\r\n<br>\r\n","caption":"Yellow Pine Road Map","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Idaho State","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"1900-01-01T05:42:27.000Z","updated_at":"2022-06-05T05:42:27.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2540","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2540"}}],"detail":{"id":"615","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"This run is more difficult at the top and gradually gets easier, making it suitable for a variety of skills and water levels. It starts out class V (IV at low levels) and ends as class III (II+ at low levels).\n\nApproximately 3 miles downstream of the confluence of Johnson Creek with the East Fork South Fork Salmon, the action begins with two long class V rapids called Flight Simulator. The first is near the road (visible 3.2 miles downstream from Yellow Pine or 0.6 mile upstream from the Sheep Creek Trailhead) and starts out easy and gets harder. The second is far down in the canyon and is hard to see from the road. In fact it is just plain hard. The standard class IV put-in is just above Caton Creek at Sheep Creek Trailhead. The road is nearby for the rest of the run and roadside scouting is easy. The river is active and some of the drops can be IV+ with enough water in them. Four miles below Caton Creek, after six miles of class IV, Deadman Campground makes a good take-out, put-in, lunch spot. The next five miles to the take-out are gradually easier class III, but you may chose to portage once or twice. You can continue down the South Fork Salmon to the end of the road for another four miles of class II if you like.\n\nLogistics: Set your navigation to Yellow Pine and determine what option is best to get there. You can come down Johnson Creek and approach the run from the upstream end. More commonly paddlers will come in on the Warm Lake Road and South Fork Salmon Road from Cascade (50 miles of paved roads) or the Lick Creek Road from McCall (35 mile dirt road) to approach the run from the downstream end. This option allows you to scout the entire run by driving the shuttle road on river right that offers a multitude of access points and easy road scouting.\n\nTo get to the take-out: From the South Fork Salmon Road (paved) go up the gravel road toward Yellow Pine less than a half mile, cross the bridge and park.\n\nIntermediate put-ins: Deadman, Caton Creek, and SheepCreek Trailhead are marked by small wooden signs.\n\nTo get to the put-in: From MP 47 continue upstream three quarters of a mile past the ranch/development to a dirt side road and park up the road (away from the river). This road is two and one half miles below the intersection of Yellow Pine Ave and Stibnite Rd.\n\nBe careful at high water:\n\nhttp://youtu.be/gAtAFYkjVmw","averageGradient":"75","maxGradient":null,"length":"15","correlations":[{"reachID":"615","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"300","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"1000"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"JOHNSON CREEK AT YELLOW PINE ID","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"13313000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"2410","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"5.42","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"44.96166667","longitude":"-115.5"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/13313000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/13313000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"above-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"2410","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["XsfQWCg0wUNrzM85vVUN0","107572","107573","107574","116623"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-115.50087,44.962273],[-115.501006,44.962422],[-115.501156,44.962483],[-115.501456,44.96249],[-115.5018,44.962429],[-115.502003,44.962346],[-115.502197,44.962316],[-115.502379,44.962361],[-115.502443,44.962429],[-115.502443,44.962543],[-115.502443,44.962543],[-115.503141,44.962566],[-115.504799,44.962561],[-115.504799,44.962561],[-115.505512,44.962559],[-115.506595,44.962657],[-115.507293,44.962862],[-115.508237,44.963105],[-115.50873,44.963098],[-115.509385,44.963029],[-115.510318,44.962893],[-115.511016,44.962688],[-115.512464,44.9623],[-115.512711,44.962171],[-115.512893,44.961906],[-115.512947,44.96167],[-115.513204,44.961435],[-115.514148,44.960919],[-115.514545,44.96063],[-115.51505,44.96019],[-115.51549,44.96],[-115.516005,44.959841],[-115.516659,44.959757],[-115.517839,44.959689],[-115.517839,44.959689],[-115.517946,44.959681],[-115.518301,44.959621],[-115.518644,44.959446],[-115.519588,44.958922],[-115.520092,44.958664],[-115.520446,44.958505],[-115.520768,44.95845